# Processes as data, and the Arrow interface said once.
#
# `examples/arrows.braid` shows that Control.Arrow's INTERFACE is just
# Braid's syntax: `arr f` is any pure program, `f >>> g` is `f ; g`,
# `first f` is `f _`, `f *** g` is `f g`, `app` is `ev`. That covers
# every arrow whose category is the one Braid already computes in.
#
# The effect-shaped arrows are not new categories either: the state
# arrow is a threaded `resource`, the Kleisli/Maybe arrow is the railway
# sum track, the writer arrow is a Log resource, nondeterminism is List.
# Only IO is irreducible, and it is a GRADE on the arrow (`=IO>`) rather
# than an arrow of its own (design-effects.md).
#
# What is left over is arrows that are PROCESSES: Yampa's signal
# functions, Hughes' stream transducers, circuits. Those are a different
# category, and in Braid they are an ordinary recursive data type whose
# composition is an ordinary word.
#
# ---- the theory abstracts over the category -------------------------
# A theory parameter may be a type CONSTRUCTOR:
# `theory Arrow(k(..., ...))`, with the arity written as marks because a
# bare name already means a wire and `...` already means a stack.  `k`
# is not a type and never reaches inference.  It lives in the slot
# signatures, and the model head names a declared data type that is
# substituted for it before any slot is checked.  That is the ML-functor
# move rather than higher kinds: `Ty` has no constructor variable, and
# nothing is inferred or dispatched.  Slots carry their OWN variables
# (`a b c` below), each generalized per slot.

# ---- the hom-object ranges over STACKS ------------------------------
# `data Circuit(a..., b...)` writes each parameter with a trailing `...`,
# which makes it a whole stack, so a circuit is a process on a whole SIDE
# of a diagram rather than on one wire.  A wide stage embeds as itself,
# so the doctrine needs no pairing parameter, no `data Pair` to be it,
# and no strength `first`.
#
# The step function's two stacks ride in a `Box`, the prelude's anonymous
# product `data Box(...) = (...)`.  That is a REPRESENTATION choice and
# it stays inside the carrier: `Circuit(a, b)`'s arguments are the stacks
# themselves, and `arrC`'s declared slot is `Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ ⇒ k(a, b)` with no
# box in sight.  A codata carrier needs the box because its step leaves
# the successor BESIDE the output, and a passenger beside an OPEN stack
# is unwritable: `...` covers the top, so nothing can be pushed above it
# (guide-open-arity.md, Rule 2).  `Frame`'s column store boxes its rows
# for the same reason.
data Circuit(a..., b...) = Fn⟨Box(a) =Recursive> Box(b) Circuit(a, b)⟩

# The step function is `=Recursive>`: every circuit below is written
# under `with Recursive`, and a written arrow means its manifest
# (MANUAL §14). The slots say so too: a slot declares the MOST a model
# may do, and the pure model below passes under a `=Recursive>` slot by
# absorption.
#
# What the slots DO NOT say is anything about the PROGRAM handed to
# `embed`. Composition joins grades, so `arrC`, which ties a knot, does
# not demand that its argument recurse. `Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩` is what the embedding
# takes, and `Arr` wraps the same pure arrow.

# `Fn` itself cannot fill `k`: it is built in, it takes an ARROW rather
# than two wires, and a constructor parameter must name a declared data
# type. One line of wrapping buys it, and gives the theory a second,
# deliberately boring model to be audited against.
data Arr(a..., b...) = Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩

# ---- the theory ------------------------------------------------------
# `in Doctrine` is the whole of it. `Doctrine` is the prelude's theory of
# arrows, with `compose`, `embed` and the five category laws, and a theory
# that says `in Doctrine` DECLARES that its slots of those names are the
# doctrine's. The elaborator checks the claim against the written
# signatures and then has, without reading a shape,
# without reading a shape or guessing, that `with Circuits` transports.
#
# Before this the three slots were DETECTED by matching arrow shapes,
# and the names were this file's taste. Detection answered "does this
# theory happen to look like a category"; the declaration answers "does
# it claim to be one", which is the question `in` was invented for.
#
# `compose` is the slot that makes constructor parameters necessary:
# its type mentions `k` at three different pairs of STACKS, which a
# wire parameter could not have said.
#
# `observe` and `sample` are the audit's evidence, and they are the
# doctrine's slots too. A circuit is codata, so it cannot be compared,
# only OBSERVED; the doctrine asks for the observation, which is what
# keeps its laws finite tests of an infinite object.
# A theory that declares neither gets no laws; see `Vault` below.
theory Arrow(k(..., ...)) in Doctrine =
    embed   : Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ =Recursive> k(a, b)
    compose : k(a, b) k(b, c) =Recursive> k(a, c)
    observe : k(Int, Int) =Recursive> Int
    sample  : • =Recursive> k(Int, Int)

# ---- model one: circuits ---------------------------------------------
# A circuit consumes an input and returns an output AND ITS SUCCESSOR.
# Recursion through Fn is what makes it codata (cf. examples/stream.braid).
# One step, boxed in the carrier's own spelling, and one step at the
# width the user sees, which boxes and unboxes around it.
def stepB = (c x -> c ; unCircuit ; _ x ; ev)
def stepC = _ Box ; stepB ; swap ; _ unBox
#   stepC : Circuit(ρ, σ) ρ =Recursive> Circuit(ρ, σ) σ

# `arr` and `>>>`.  No `first`: a wide stage is an ordinary stage now,
# and the identity is `embed [pass]`, which needs no slot and never
# did.
def arrC with Recursive = (g -> [(x -> x ; unBox ; g ... ; ev ; Box ; _ (g ; arrC))] ; Circuit)
def compC with Recursive = (f g -> [(x -> f x ; stepB ; (y f2 -> g y ; stepB ; (z g2 -> z (f2 g2 ; compC))))] ; Circuit)
def idC = [pass] ; arrC

# A circuit that CANNOT be a pure function: its output depends on every
# input it has already seen. This is the whole reason Arrows exist.
def sumFrom with Recursive = (n -> [(x -> n (x ; unBox) ; + ; dup ; (s t -> (s ; Box) (t ; sumFrom)))] ; Circuit)
def scale with Recursive = (n -> [(x -> ((n (x ; unBox) ; *) ; Box) (n ; scale))] ; Circuit)

# feed 3 then 4, and report both outputs summed: two steps, because one
# step cannot tell a stateful circuit from a stateless one
def probe = (c -> c 3 ; stepC ; (c2 y -> c2 4 ; stepC ; (c3 z -> (y z ; +) (c3 ; drop))))

model Circuits in Arrow(Circuit) =
    embed   = arrC
    compose = compC
    observe = probe
    sample  = 0 ; sumFrom

# ---- model two: plain functions --------------------------------------
# The boring model, and the reason for having one: the same five laws
# run again over a carrier with no state at all.
def arrA     = Arr
def runA     = unArr ... ; ev
def thenA    = (f g -> [f ... ; runA ; g ... ; runA] ; Arr)
def observeA = (f -> f 3 ; runA ; (y -> f 4 ; runA ; (z -> y z ; +)))

model Funcs in Arrow(Arr) =
    embed   = arrA
    compose = thenA
    observe = observeA
    sample  = [dup ; +] ; Arr

# ---- generic code, written once --------------------------------------
# The body is the same twice; only the model differs.  `in` puts the
# model's slot words in scope and APPLIES NOTHING, so resolution is once per
# scope, never a dictionary per call.
def twiceC in Circuits = sample sample ; compose ; observe
def twiceA in Funcs = sample sample ; compose ; observe

twiceC ; print              # 13  — a running sum fed into a running sum:
                            #       3 ⇒ 3 ⇒ 3, then 4 ⇒ 7 ⇒ 10
twiceA ; print              # 28  — doubling twice: 3⋅4 + 4⋅4

# and the composite really is stateful: sum, then double
(0 ; sumFrom) (2 ; scale) ; compC
_ 3  ; stepC ; (c y -> c (y ; print))      # 6
_ 4  ; stepC ; (c y -> c (y ; print))      # 14
_ 10 ; stepC ; (c y -> c (y ; print))      # 34
drop

# ---- composition, OWNED: `with` a model that has a carrier -------------
# `in Circuits` is a VOCABULARY: it puts this model's `embed` and
# `compose` in scope and leaves the plumbing to you.  `with Circuits`
# goes one step further and takes over `;`.
#
# The theory above DECLARES that: `in Doctrine`, plus the two structure
# slots at the doctrine's signatures, so `compose` is `k(ρ,σ) k(σ,τ) ⇒
# k(ρ,τ)` and `embed` is `Fn⟨ρ ⇒ σ⟩ ⇒ k(ρ,σ)`, and that is the whole
# test.  The names are the
# doctrine's, which is why they are these names and not others: one
# spelling per thing.  `with Circuits` is then the functor out of the free
# category that sends every stage to the embedding of that stage and
# every `;` to the composition.  It is a LABEL WITH A CARRIER: it mints
# the receipt `Circuits` exactly as any functor scope does, and the
# carrier is the hom-object `Circuit(a, b)`.  The display folds the two
# together: a transported word is `• ⇒ Circuit(Int, Int)` carrying
# `Circuits` at the base, and prints `Int =Circuits Recursive> Int`.  Objects
# are shared with the base category; composition is the model's own.

def add1 = _ 1 ; +
def dbl  = 2 _ ; *

# The same pipeline twice.  `hand` is what `with` writes out for you;
# `easy` is what you write.
def hand in Circuits = [add1] ... ; embed ... ; _ [dbl] ; _ embed ; compose
def easy with Circuits = add1 ; dbl
#   easy : Int =Circuits Recursive> Int

# ENTERING is a marker, LEAVING is a model.  `observe` takes the carrier
# and returns base, an EXIT read off its declared arrow, so it is
# refused inside `with Circuits` ("`observe` leaves Circuits") and called
# under `in Circuits`, where nothing is transported.  That rule is what
# makes the word table exact: every word written under `with` or `in`
# produces a carrier unless it deliberately exits, so the elaborator
# never has to ask inference which is which.
def handOut in Circuits = hand ; observe
def easyOut in Circuits = easy ; observe

handOut ; print             # 18 — (3+1)·2 + (4+1)·2
easyOut ; print             # 18 — the same pipeline, transported

# A transported word is an ordinary word, so it composes in the category
# with any other: inside `with Circuits`, `easy` is left alone and the
# `;` around it is `compose`.
def twice2 with Circuits = easy ; easy
def twice2Out in Circuits = twice2 ; observe
twice2Out ; print           # 40 — 3 ⇒ 8 ⇒ 18, 4 ⇒ 10 ⇒ 22

# OUTSIDE the scope, two of them side by side are two circuits side by
# side: `• =Circuits Recursive> Circuit(Int, Int) Circuit(Int, Int)`.  That
# is well-typed and visibly not composition in Circuits.  The display fold
# folds ONE carrier, so it does not fire and both are visible.
# Composition is written under the marker.
def pair    = easy easy
def pairOut in Circuits = pair ; compose ; observe
pairOut ; print             # 40 — the same composite, spelled by hand

# ---- wider stages: there is nothing to say ---------------------------
# A hom-object `k(ρ, σ)` names a whole STACK on each side, so a
# transported spine is one carrier wire whose two objects are the base
# stacks themselves.  A stage of any width embeds as ITSELF, so `embed
# [dup]` is `Circuit(Int, Int Int)`, and the base's own discipline
# (every stage covers the stack it is handed; `_` and `...` say how)
# rides through the functor untouched.
#
# A one-wire hom-object would need a packing and a whiskering
# loop.  Both existed because the carrier was one wire.
#
# So `dup ; *` transports, and squares:
def sq with Circuits = dup ; *
#   sq : Int =Circuits Recursive> Int
def sqOut in Circuits = sq ; observe
sqOut ; print               # 25 — 3² + 4²

# three wires, and a stage on a wire that is not the deepest
def poly with Circuits = dup ; _ dup ; _ _ add1 ; _ * ; +
#   poly : Int =Circuits Recursive> Int, which is x + x·(x+1)
def polyOut in Circuits = poly ; observe
polyOut ; print             # 39 — (3 + 3·4) + (4 + 4·5)

# ---- a carrier built BY HAND: `in Circuits` -------------------------
# `sumFrom` is the reason the Arrow exists: a stateful circuit that is not
# `embed` of anything.  Without a written header, a def like `0 ; sumFrom`
# would be STRANDED.  It produces a `Circuit(Int, Int)` at the base, and
# the elaborator will not treat it as a word of the category on that
# evidence, because doing so would mean reading an INFERRED type to decide
# what a word is.
#
# `in Circuits` is the written declaration that closes it.  It applies
# nothing, with no embedding, no composition and no transport.  It says "this
# def is a morphism of the category Circuits presents", and the
# elaborator CHECKS the claim against the def's arrow: a word of a
# category builds one carrier out of nothing.  The claim is written, the
# check is mechanical, and the def joins the model's word table beside
# the ones `with Circuits` transported.
def sum0 in Circuits = 0 ; sumFrom
#   sum0 : • =Recursive> Circuit(Int, Int), UNFOLDED and with no `Circuits` on it.
#
# A receipt is PROVENANCE: `=Circuits>` says the code went through the
# functor.  `sum0` did not.  It is a circuit no base program denotes,
# which is the reason Arrows exist, so claiming the label would claim
# membership in the functor's IMAGE, which is strictly stronger.  `in`
# mints nothing, because `in` applies nothing.  Only a `with` mints, and a
# `with` mints when it CHANGED the code.
# Membership is carried by the carrier in the type and the entry in the
# word table.
#
# Under the marker it composes like any other word of the category, and
# THAT composite went through the functor, so it folds:
def running with Circuits = sum0 ; dbl
#   running : Int =Circuits Recursive> Int
def runningOut in Circuits = running ; observe
runningOut ; print          # 20 — 3 ⇒ 3 ⇒ 6, then 4 ⇒ 7 ⇒ 14

# ---- over/use coherence, as a law -------------------------------------
# `with` writes out what `in` writes by hand, so the two must denote
# the same morphism.  Here is the statement at a stateful pair, where
# the claim has teeth: `sum0` and `scale3` are both built by hand.
def scale3 in Circuits = 3 ; scale

def byHand in Circuits = sum0 scale3 ; compose
def byUse with Circuits = sum0 ; scale3
#   byHand : • =Recursive> Circuit(Int, Int)   no label: nothing was applied
#   byUse  : Int =Circuits Recursive> Int      transported, so it folds

def handObs in Circuits = byHand ; observe
def useObs in Circuits = byUse  ; observe
handObs ; print             # 30 — 3 ⇒ 3 ⇒ 9, then 4 ⇒ 7 ⇒ 21
useObs  ; print             # 30 — the same circuit, said two ways

# `sameCode` cannot DECIDE it, for the reason the laws hit below:
# composing circuits applies a program that arrived as a wire, and `ev` of
# a value that is not a literal quotation is outside the structural
# fragment.  Coherence is therefore observed rather than proved, which is
# why `observe` is a slot of the theory.

# ---- the corollary: a SEALED category ---------------------------------
# Exits are the only way out, so a theory that declares none is sealed:
# every stage inside the scope is embedded and the only word that
# touches the accumulated carrier is the composition, which hands back a
# carrier.  Nothing written in the category can leave it, and that is
# abstract-type sealing falling out of the same rule.
data Capsule(a..., b...) = Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩

theory Vault(k(..., ...)) in Doctrine =
    embed   : Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ ⇒ k(a, b)
    compose : k(a, b) k(b, c) ⇒ k(a, c)

def runCap  = unCapsule ... ; ev
def capThen = (f g -> [f ... ; runCap ; g ... ; runCap] ; Capsule)

model Sealed in Vault(Capsule) =
    embed   = Capsule
    compose = capThen

def guarded with Sealed = add1 ; dbl
def guarded2 with Sealed = guarded ; add1
#   guarded2 : Int =Sealed> Int, and the only consumer of a Sealed
#   word, inside the category's vocabulary, is another Sealed word.
#
# `Vault` takes the doctrine's `compose` and `embed`, which is the whole
# interface, so `def wide with Sealed = dup ; *` transports too, at no
# extra cost to the model.  The level table has two rows: compose alone
# composes carriers by hand, and compose with embed transports ANY stage.
#
# It declares no `observe` and no `sample`, so NO doctrine law runs for
# `Sealed`.  An inherited law runs when the model can state it, and a
# sealed category admits no observation.  That is the reading of sealed,
# and it is worth seeing beside `Circuits`, where all five run.

# The caveat: the CARRIER's own unroller is an ordinary base word and
# Braid has no export lists, so `unCapsule` still opens it from outside.
# A model seals the CATEGORY, so nothing written in it leaves it by any
# route the model offers, and sealing the TYPE is a separate question the
# language does not answer.
guarded2 ; unCapsule ; _ 5 ; ev ; print    # 13 — ((5+1)·2)+1

# ---- what the laws can and cannot decide ------------------------------
# The five category axioms, leftId, rightId, assoc, embedFunctor and
# embedWide, are the PRELUDE's, inherited by `in Doctrine` and run for
# `Circuits` and for `Funcs`.  The doctrine writes them once.
#
# They run through `observe` at sample points, and that is the ONLY way
# they run.  A one-wire hom-object would make some of them provable:
# `Arr(a, b)` would unroll to `Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩` at one wire on each side, so the
# normalizer could give the `ev` inside `thenA` an arity.  Over STACKS it
# cannot: `Arr(ρ, σ)` unrolls to `Fn⟨ρ ⇒ σ⟩` with ρ open, and `ev` of an
# open wire has no closed arity, which is the wall `Len` stands at in
# `examples/transformations.braid`.  That is the price of flat products:
# what a one-wire hom-object buys is a decidable `ev`, and what it costs
# is a pairing, a `data Pair` per file and a routing pass.
#
# `Circuits` proves none of them, for a structural reason.  `compC`
# applies the self wire of a `fix`, whose arrow is `Fn⟨Σ =Recursive> Θ⟩`
# with Σ an open stack variable, so there is no arity to give THAT either.
# Equality modulo a model's defining equations is structural induction
# over an initial algebra, a different procedure from normalizing in a
# free category (design-macros.md, "honest gaps").  For codata the laws
# stay finite tests of an infinite object, which is what `observe` was
# asked for in the first place.

# The laws ran before any of this printed: five of them, twice, for two
# models with nothing in common but the doctrine.  `embedWide` is the one
# worth a second look.  It says `embed [f] ; embed [g] = embed [f ; g]` at
# a stage that is one wire in and TWO out, and at one that whiskers,
# `(_ 1 ; +) _`, a stage on the deepest wire with one riding above.  There
# is no strength to state a law about, because the whiskering happens in
# the BASE, inside the quotation, before `embed` ever sees it.
#
# Stating `compose` needs `k` at three different pairs of STACKS,
# `k(ρ, σ)`, `k(σ, τ)` and `k(ρ, τ)`, which a wire parameter could not
# say.  That is why a one-object `theory Pipeline(a)` is a category with
# ONE object and this one is not.
